  "content": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://infosec.exchange/@sudorandom\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"\u003e@\u003cspan\u003esudorandom\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e in the old days, when folk sat down with a paper copy of K\u0026amp;R, it was always the chapter in pointers. People who'd been exposed to assembly had no problem, but otherwise that was the real challenge.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOn a more advanced level and personally, things like the Chinese Remainder Theorem, Galois Fields, etc. I'm not a natural mathematician.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tech.lgbt/tags/DailyPrompt\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"\u003e#\u003cspan\u003eDailyPrompt\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e \u003ca href=\"https://tech.lgbt/tags/softwareengineering\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"\u003e#\u003cspan\u003esoftwareengineering\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
